## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft branchless bit-twiddling in 128-bit space, a common low-level optimization task.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit integer handling via two 64-bit halves
- Trailing-zero count (ctz) tricks without branches
- De Bruijn sequences for fast bit indexing

## Code Example
```c
unsigned countTrailingZeros128(uint128 x) {
  static const unsigned char deBruijn64[64] = {
    0, 1, 2, 53, 3, 7, 54, 27,
    4, 38, 8, 34, 55, 48, 28, 20,
    5, 39, 53, 6, 59, 29, 42, 16,
    9, 35, 60, 50, 45, 27, 21, 17,
    10, 49, 56, 40, 30, 14, 43, 15,
    11, 23, 51, 52, 41, 19, 13, 44,
    12, 26, 58, 36, 31, 37, 29, 22
  };
  unsigned loIdx = ((x.lo & -x.lo) * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ull) >> 58;
  unsigned hiIdx = ((x.hi & -x.hi) * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ull) >> 58;
  unsigned tzLo = deBruijn64[loIdx];
  unsigned tzHi = deBruijn64[hiIdx];
  unsigned isLoZero = (x.lo == 0);
  return (1 - isLoZero) * tzLo + isLoZero * (64 + tzHi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support a variable-width 128-bit type with non-power-of-two lanes?
- Compare performance of this approach against a masked binary search approach on modern CPUs.

## Why This Is Asked

- Checks 128-bit handling and branchless bit counting, a common crypto/DB pattern.
- Evaluates ability to compose primitives without loops.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit chunks
- Splitting 128-bit into hi/lo and aggregating results
- Constant-time, no branches

## Code Example

```javascript
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement a constant-time popcount-style operation using only masks and shifts, simulating a common bitmap diff in games or databases.

## Key Concepts
- Hamming weight via parallel reduction
- XOR to identify differences
- Mask sequences for bit counting

## Code Example
```cpp
int hammingDistance64(uint64 a, uint64 b){
  uint64 x = a ^ b;
  x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) + ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL);
  x = (x & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00ff00ff00ff00ffULL);
  x = (x & 0x0000ffff0000ffffULL) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000ffff0000ffffULL);
  x = (x & 0x00000000ffffffffULL) + ((x >> 32) & 0x00000000ffffffffULL);
  return (int)x;
}
```

