- How would you adapt the approach for 128-bit inputs?
- What are the trade-offs vs builtin popcount on modern CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses branch-free 128-bit rotation, a common primitive in crypto and low-level optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values via two 64-bit halves
- Branchless selection with masks
- Cross-half rotation logic and edge cases (n=0, n=64, n=127)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, uint8_t n) {
  uint64_t n0 = n & 63;
  uint64_t m  = -((int)((n >> 6) & 1)); // 0 or -1
  uint64_t hi1 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo1 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t hi2 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo2 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (hi1 & ~m) | (hi2 & m);
  r.lo = (lo1 & ~m) | (lo2 & m);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How might you test for constant-time properties across inputs?
- How would you extend to a constant-time generic rotate for arbitrary bit-widths?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to decompose a 128‑bit value into four 32‑bit lanes and re‑assemble them without branches.

## Key Concepts
- Lane extraction via masks and shifts
- 128‑bit as hi/lo pairs with 32‑bit lanes
- Branchless permutation and safe packing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 permute32x4_128(uint128 x) {
  uint32 A = (uint32)(x.hi >> 32);
  uint32 B = (uint32)(x.hi & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  uint32 C = (uint32)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32 D = (uint32)(x.lo & 0xFFFFFFFF);
  uint128 y;
  y.hi = ((uint64_t)B << 32) | A;
  y.lo = ((uint64_t)D << 32) | C;
  return y;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to permute eight 16‑bit lanes within 128‑bit? 
- What tests validate boundary conditions (e.g., when A/B/C/D are all zeros or all ones)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to implement a branchless, correct popcount in O(1) time, a common low-level optimization in performance-critical code (DBs, networking) at senior-level shops like Oracle and Twitter.

## Key Concepts
- Branchless bit counting
- Parallel bit manipulation with masks
- 32-bit mask progression and final accumulation

## Code Example
```c
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x){
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  return x & 0x3F;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit inputs?
- Compare with hardware POPCNT; what are portability trade-offs?
- How would you test exhaustively or with randomized tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of 128-bit bitboards, branchless kth-one, and cross-half boundary without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value as two 64-bit words; popcount and select in constant time
- Branchless kth-one within a 64-bit word
- Correct handling when n is out of range across halves

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
int findNthOne128(uint128 a, unsigned n){
  unsigned loCnt = __builtin_popcountll(a.lo);
  unsigned hiCnt = __builtin_popcountll(a.hi);
  unsigned total = loCnt + hiCnt;
  if (n >= total) return -1;
  if (n < loCnt) return kth1_64(a.lo, n);
  return 64 + kth1_64(a.hi, n - loCnt);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 256-bit values without branches?
- What are trade-offs across compilers for portability vs performance?

