## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply SWAR-style bit counting across a 128-bit structure without branches, using two independent 64-bit halves. Also checks portability when no native 128-bit constant exists.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR popcount on 64-bit chunks
- 128-bit represented as { hi, lo }
- Edge-case handling for 0 and all-ones

## Code Example

```c
uint8_t popcount128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo = lo - ((lo >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  lo = (lo & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((lo >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  lo = (lo + (lo >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  lo = lo + (lo >> 8);
  lo = lo + (lo >> 16);
  lo = lo + (lo >> 32);

  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi = hi - ((hi >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  hi = (hi & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((hi >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  hi = (hi + (hi >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  hi = hi + (hi >> 8);
  hi = hi + (hi >> 16);
  hi = hi + (hi >> 32);

  uint8_t cl = lo & 0x7F;
  uint8_t ch = hi & 0x7F;
  return cl + ch;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for wider word sizes (e.g., 256-bit)?
- Could compiler intrinsics offer portable fallbacks here?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests depth in bit twiddling and constant-time operations; parity computation is fundamental in checksums, error detection, cryptography, and performance-critical code paths.

## Key Concepts
- Parity reduction across word boundaries by folding upper and lower halves
- Fixed-time parallel reduction using shifts and XOR
- No branches; constant-time execution regardless of input

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t p = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  p ^= p >> 32;
  p ^= p >> 16;
  p ^= p >> 8;
  p ^= p >> 4;
  p ^= p >> 2;
  p ^= p >> 1;
  return p & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to arbitrary bit widths or for streaming parity computation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-level data packing and word-level efficiency.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (dilating)
- Inline masks and shifts for 32-bit results
- Morton codes for spatial indexing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t part1by1(uint16_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x &= 0x0000FFFF;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t morton2D(uint16_t x, uint16_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 3D Morton codes (x,y,z) and 64-bit result? 
- How would you adapt for non-power-of-two bit widths or streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked

Demonstrates capability to manipulate multi-word integers with pure bit tricks, a common requirement in crypto and low-level systems programming. It tests cross-lane thinking and branch-free implementation.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-lane bit propagation between hi and lo.
- Branch-free 128-bit rotation using only shifts and OR.

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft1281(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = (x.lo << 1) | (x.hi >> 63);
  r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to rotate left by k (0 <= k < 128) without branches?
- How would you test boundary cases like k=0, k=127, across hi/lo boundaries?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a branchless, constant-time population-count across 128 bits using only shifts and masks, a common low-level optimization in crypto/signature code.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register)
- 64-bit popcount, extended to 128-bit
- Branchless arithmetic for performance

## Code Example

```c
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t w) {
  w = w - ((w >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  w = (w & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((w >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  w = (w + (w >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (unsigned int)((w * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56);
}
unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  unsigned int hi = popcount64(x.hi);
  unsigned int lo = popcount64(x.lo);
  return hi + lo;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for variable-width counts (e.g., 256-bit)?
- What are the trade-offs compared to using hardware popcnt when available?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply bit-dilation technique and Morton code basics, a common need in graphics or spatial indexing.

## Key Concepts

- Bit spreading (dilate) with masks
- Interleaving two bit streams into a 64-bit value
- No-loops approach with fixed masks

## Code Example

```c
uint64_t morton2D64(uint32_t x, uint32_t y) {
  uint64_t dx = x;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;

  uint64_t dy = y;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;

  return dx | (dy << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to 3D Morton code with x,y,z?
- What are performance considerations on 64-bit vs 32-bit platforms and compiler optimizations?
